WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.064
A payments service keeps merchant names in Oracle, and copies of them in Redis, a cache in memory.

2
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:13.903
The rule so far: commit to the database, then delete the cached copy.

3
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:16.139
That rule still has a hole.

4
00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:20.152
Reader A, a fraud check, asks Redis for merchant m-17.

5
00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:23.049
Redis has nothing for it, so it misses.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.162
It reads the row from Oracle: version 1, with the old name.

7
00:00:27.162 --> 00:00:31.499
Then it stalls before storing it, say in a garbage collection pause.

8
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.952
Meanwhile, the writer renames the merchant and commits version 2.

9
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.084
Then it deletes the cached copy, as the rule says.

10
00:00:39.084 --> 00:00:42.409
There's nothing there yet, so the delete removes nothing.

11
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.111
Now reader A's pause ends, and it stores what it read: version 1, kept for an hour.

12
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.550
Reader B asks for the merchant and gets the old name.

13
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.271
So does every request for the next hour, and no write is coming to delete it.

14
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.743
This is a stale set.

15
00:00:57.743 --> 00:01:03.276
Reader A's read came before the commit, and its write to the cache came after the delete.

16
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:05.256
The race is rare per request.

17
00:01:05.256 --> 00:01:10.913
When Meta measured, about one cache write in a million was still wrong five minutes later.

18
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:15.594
Redis forgot the delete, because deleting an empty key leaves no trace.

19
00:01:15.594 --> 00:01:18.421
The fix gives the delete something to cancel.

20
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:19.534
Run it again.

21
00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:26.327
On the miss, reader A also takes the key's lease: a random token, stored in Redis next to the key.

22
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.958
Reader A reads version 1, then stalls.

23
00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:33.873
The writer commits version 2, then deletes the value and the lease together.

24
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:37.449
Reader A's pause ends, and it tries to fill the key.

25
00:01:37.449 --> 00:01:41.936
A small script checks for its token and stores the value in one step.

26
00:01:41.936 --> 00:01:44.668
The token is gone, so the fill is refused.

27
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:50.719
Reader B misses, takes its own lease, reads version 2, and fills the cache with the new name.

28
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:56.112
Every write into the cache must hold the key's lease, and every delete takes the lease away.
